🔍 Feature Selection

What is Feature Selection?

**Feature selection** is the process of selecting the most **relevant features (variables)** from a dataset that contribute the most to predicting the target variable.

---

Why Feature Selection is Important?

* Reduces overfitting
* Improves model accuracy
* Reduces training time
* Enhances model interpretability
* Removes irrelevant and redundant features

---

Types of Feature Selection Methods

1. **Filter Methods**
2. Wrapper Methods
3. Embedded Methods

👉 **ANOVA and Chi-Square are Filter Methods**

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧪 Feature Selection using ANOVA

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

What is ANOVA (in Feature Selection)?

**ANOVA (Analysis of Variance)** is used to measure the **relationship between numerical features and a categorical target**.

It checks whether the **means of a feature differ significantly across target classes**.

---

When to Use ANOVA?

* Feature is **continuous (numerical)**
* Target variable is **categorical**
* Classes are independent

---

Intuition Behind ANOVA

* If feature values vary **significantly across classes**, the feature is important
* If feature values are similar across classes, the feature is less useful

---

ANOVA in Feature Selection

* Uses **F-score**
* Higher F-score → stronger relationship with target
* Features ranked based on F-score

---

Key Assumptions

* Normal distribution within groups
* Equal variances (homoscedasticity)
* Independent samples

---

Advantages

* Simple and fast
* Works well for numerical data
* Scales to large datasets

---

Limitations

* Captures only linear relationships
* Sensitive to outliers
* Assumptions may not always hold

---

Typical Use Cases

* Classification problems
* Text classification (after numeric encoding)
* Feature ranking before model training

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧮 Feature Selection using Chi-Square Test

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

What is Chi-Square Feature Selection?

The **Chi-Square test** measures the **dependence between a categorical feature and a categorical target**.

It checks whether the observed frequency differs significantly from expected frequency.

---

When to Use Chi-Square?

* Feature is **categorical**
* Target variable is **categorical**
* Feature values are **non-negative**
* Works best with count/frequency data

---

Intuition Behind Chi-Square

* If feature and target are independent → feature is not useful
* Strong dependency → feature is important

---

Chi-Square in Feature Selection

* Uses **χ² score**
* Higher χ² value → stronger association
* Features ranked by χ² score

---

Key Assumptions

* Expected frequency ≥ 5
* Independent observations
* Categorical data only

---

Advantages

* Very fast
* Works well for text data (bag-of-words, TF)
* Easy to interpret

---

Limitations

* Cannot handle continuous data directly
* Sensitive to low frequencies
* Ignores feature interactions

---

Typical Use Cases

* Text classification
* Categorical datasets
* Feature selection for Naive Bayes

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔁 ANOVA vs Chi-Square (Feature Selection)

| Aspect           | ANOVA             | Chi-Square          |
| ---------------- | ----------------- | ------------------- |
| Feature Type     | Numerical         | Categorical         |
| Target Type      | Categorical       | Categorical         |
| Measures         | Mean difference   | Dependency          |
| Score Used       | F-score           | χ² score            |
| Data Requirement | Continuous        | Non-negative counts |
| Common Use       | ML classification | Text analytics      |

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚙️ Feature Selection Workflow

1. Identify feature types
2. Choose appropriate method (ANOVA / Chi-Square)
3. Compute scores
4. Rank features
5. Select top-k features
6. Train model with selected features

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚠️ Important Notes

* Feature selection should be done **after train-test split**
* Avoid data leakage
* Feature importance ≠ causation
* Combine with domain knowledge

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Best Practices

* Use ANOVA for numerical features
* Use Chi-Square for categorical/text features
* Normalize data if required
* Validate selected features with models

